            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nBefore you start, we recommend reading *Learning Multiplication Table (2 and 3)* (<htcite0>), where you learned how to multiply by two and by three.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "8bbd4fa3-d3cf-4ff4-bd96-65dc852e508b",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Learning Multiplication Table (2 and 3)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nA multiplication table is like a map of answers. When we know the map, we do not need to count everything again. Today we will learn two more maps: the table of four (4) and the table of five (5).",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To learn the table of four, we will look at squares and rectangles. To learn the table of five, we will use our hands. Let's begin!",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Squares and rectangles</b><br /><br />Pause and think: how many sides does a square have? And how many sides does a rectangle have?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_four",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Four (4)\n\nA square has four equal sides (<htcite0>). A rectangle also has four sides (<htcite1>). Look at Figure 1: the square and the rectangle look different, but both have four sides.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c0c1967a-f27a-4191-82f1-c5e44c44a297",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W., Square",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3b7542b4-d24a-4708-a890-e2c57fd0f1ae",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W., Rectangle",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 430 150\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A square and a rectangle\"><g stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"30\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\"/><rect x=\"200\" y=\"20\" width=\"200\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#e3f2fd\" stroke=\"#1565c0\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 1</b>: A square (left) and a rectangle (right). Both have four sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Counting the Sides of Squares\n\nOne square has 4 sides. Two squares have 4 + 4 = 8 sides. Three squares have 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 sides. We are counting by fours: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20...",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"imgcenterresponsive\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 550 140\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five squares of the same size\"><g fill=\"#e8f5e9\" stroke=\"#2e7d32\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"450\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"/></g></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Five squares of the same size, each with four equal sides.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: Sides of squares</b><br />Look at Figure 2: it shows 5 squares, and together they have 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 20 sides. Each square has 4 sides. Suppose we want to count all the sides of a group of squares. Try different numbers of squares:<br />• 1 square: 4 × 1 = 4 sides.<br />• 2 squares: 4 × 2 = 8 sides.<br />• 4 squares: 4 × 4 = 16 sides.<br />• 5 squares: 4 × 5 = 20 sides.<br />Do you see the pattern? Each new square adds 4 more sides. That is what we call counting by fours: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20!</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A Formula for the Table of Four\n\nWe can write this rule as a formula that works for every number **N**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>4</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Multiplying any number by four is the same as adding it four times.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- Read it aloud: “four times N equals N plus N plus N plus N.”\n- The answer is always N added to itself four times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by four at once.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 4.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now try it yourself: hover the mouse over each multiplication in the box on the left. As you do, the table on the right highlights the matching row, so you can watch every answer for the table of four appear.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent4\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Sides in five squares</b><br /><br />Pause and think: if you have 5 squares, how many sides do you have in all?<br /><br />(Hint: count by fours five times: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20.)</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer to our reflection: five squares are five groups of four sides, so 5 × 4 = 20 sides. Counting by fours helped us find the answer quickly. Well done!",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_five",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Multiplying by Five (5)\n\nLook at your hands. Each hand has five fingers. One hand shows 5 fingers. Two hands show 5 + 5 = 10 fingers. We are counting by fives: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25...",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left hand</td><td>Right hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgLH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgLH', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"imgRH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgRH', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: Two hands, each with five fingers. The source code is available on the project's GitHub (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "240800f3-ede4-4deb-9d4e-915a3a03fa68",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Source code",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Counting the Fingers\n\nOne hand has 5 fingers. Two hands have 10 fingers. Three hands have 15 fingers. Each new hand adds 5 more fingers.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 2: Fingers in a group</b><br />Each hand has 5 fingers. Suppose a group of friends each raises one hand. We want to know how many fingers are shown in all. Try different numbers of hands:<br />• 1 hand: 5 × 1 = 5 fingers.<br />• 2 hands: 5 × 2 = 10 fingers.<br />• 4 hands: 5 × 4 = 20 fingers.<br />• 5 hands: 5 × 5 = 25 fingers.<br />Do you see the pattern? Each new hand adds 5 more fingers. That is what we call counting by fives: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25!</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### A Formula for the Table of Five\n\nWe can write this rule as a formula that works for every number **N**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>5</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Multiplying any number by five is the same as adding it five times.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "- Read it aloud: “five times N equals N plus N plus N plus N plus N.”\n- The answer is always N added to itself five times.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one rule covers every multiplication by five at once.\n- If **N** is 0, the product is 0; if **N** is 1, the product is 5.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now try it yourself: hover the mouse over each multiplication in the box on the left. As you do, the table on the right highlights the matching row, so you can watch every answer for the table of five appear.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent5\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: How much is 5 × 4?</b><br /><br />Pause and think: count by fives four times — 5, 10, 15, 20 — or use the table of five to find the answer.<br /><br />Then check: is the result the same as 4 × 5?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Here is the answer to our reflection: counting by fives four times gives 5, 10, 15, 20, so 5 × 4 = 20. And 4 × 5 = 20 too (<htcite0>). Counting by fours five times gives 4, 8, 12, 16, 20. The answer stays the same when we change the order. What a useful shortcut!",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nWe have learned two new tables:\n\n- Multiplying by 4 means adding the number four times: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20...\n- Multiplying by 5 means adding the number five times: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25...\n\nWe used squares and rectangles for the table of four, and our hands for the table of five. We also saw that 4 × 5 = 20 and 5 × 4 = 20. Changing the order of the factors does not change the product. (<htcite0>)\n\nKeep practicing: count the sides of squares by fours and the fingers of hands by fives. You will see these two tables work every time.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ae53cf73-8e7d-4a2c-80a3-1cb79b7c48bc",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
